        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 7912,
            "sid": 3,
            "position_x": 988,
            "position_y": 248,
            "width": 798,
            "height": 1116,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Adds a fourth channel - alpha, the opacity - to a three-channel image, made from its brightness.\n\nThe new channel is the AVERAGE of each pixel's three channels. So it is not a\nplain \"fully opaque\" alpha: used as opacity, bright pixels come out solid and\ndark ones see-through, and black disappears altogether. That is what you want\nfor laying light material - text, glows, a camera image of a lit figure on\nblack - over something else, so that the black background drops out.\n\nIf you want a fully opaque alpha instead, this is not the node; build the\nchannel yourself and join it on with np.concatenate.\n\nWHAT IT ACCEPTS:\nAn image as an array of height x width x channels. Anything that is not three\ndimensional is ignored (nothing is sent). An image that already has four\nchannels is taken to have its alpha and is passed through unchanged.\n\nTHE TYPE IS KEPT:\nThe image comes out in the type it came in: a uint8 image (0 to 255) stays\nuint8, with the average rounded down to a whole number; a float image stays\nfloat, with its range unchanged.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.add_alpha\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.add_alpha\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ninput:\nThe image. Receiving it triggers the node.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nimage with alpha:\nThe image with the brightness channel added as its last channel.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nnp.grid makes a 32 x 32 x 3 array - every point's x, y and z - which is\nalso a picture: x as red, rising to the right, y as green, rising down the\npicture, and z as a flat amount of blue. The metro re-sends it ten times a\nsecond, so drag z and both pictures follow.\n\nEach side has its own mgl_context that draws a sphere first, then the\nimage in front of it with mgl_image. Left: the image as it is - with no\nalpha it is solid everywhere and hides the sphere completely. Right: the\nsame image after np.add_alpha. The dark top-left corner is nearly\ntransparent, so the sphere shows through it; the bright bottom-right\ncorner is nearly solid and hides it. Raise z and the whole image\nbrightens, so it becomes more opaque everywhere.\n\nnp.shape shows the fourth channel arriving: (32, 32, 3) in, (32, 32, 4) out.\n\nThe sphere is there because a display needs something behind the image to\nshow alpha at all - over plain black, a see-through dark pixel looks just\nlike an opaque one.\n\nRELATED:\nmgl_texture and mgl_image draw arrays as pictures. np.astype changes the\nnumber type (uint8 or float). np.concatenate joins a channel you made\nyourself; np.[] slices one out again.",
                    "value_type": "string"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 790,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1100,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "string"
                }
            },
            "text": "Adds a fourth channel - alpha, the opacity - to a three-channel image, made from its brightness.\n\nThe new channel is the AVERAGE of each pixel's three channels. So it is not a\nplain \"fully opaque\" alpha: used as opacity, bright pixels come out solid and\ndark ones see-through, and black disappears altogether. That is what you want\nfor laying light material - text, glows, a camera image of a lit figure on\nblack - over something else, so that the black background drops out.\n\nIf you want a fully opaque alpha instead, this is not the node; build the\nchannel yourself and join it on with np.concatenate.\n\nWHAT IT ACCEPTS:\nAn image as an array of height x width x channels. Anything that is not three\ndimensional is ignored (nothing is sent). An image that already has four\nchannels is taken to have its alpha and is passed through unchanged.\n\nTHE TYPE IS KEPT:\nThe image comes out in the type it came in: a uint8 image (0 to 255) stays\nuint8, with the average rounded down to a whole number; a float image stays\nfloat, with its range unchanged.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.add_alpha\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.add_alpha\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ninput:\nThe image. Receiving it triggers the node.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nimage with alpha:\nThe image with the brightness channel added as its last channel.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nnp.grid makes a 32 x 32 x 3 array - every point's x, y and z - which is\nalso a picture: x as red, rising to the right, y as green, rising down the\npicture, and z as a flat amount of blue. The metro re-sends it ten times a\nsecond, so drag z and both pictures follow.\n\nEach side has its own mgl_context that draws a sphere first, then the\nimage in front of it with mgl_image. Left: the image as it is - with no\nalpha it is solid everywhere and hides the sphere completely. Right: the\nsame image after np.add_alpha. The dark top-left corner is nearly\ntransparent, so the sphere shows through it; the bright bottom-right\ncorner is nearly solid and hides it. Raise z and the whole image\nbrightens, so it becomes more opaque everywhere.\n\nnp.shape shows the fourth channel arriving: (32, 32, 3) in, (32, 32, 4) out.\n\nThe sphere is there because a display needs something behind the image to\nshow alpha at all - over plain black, a see-through dark pixel looks just\nlike an opaque one.\n\nRELATED:\nmgl_texture and mgl_image draw arrays as pictures. np.astype changes the\nnumber type (uint8 or float). np.concatenate joins a channel you made\nyourself; np.[] slices one out again."
        },
